dmenu - dynamic menu
====================
dmenu is an efficient dynamic menu for X.


Requirements
------------
In order to build dmenu you need the Xlib header files.


Installation
------------
Edit config.mk to match your local setup (dmenu is installed into
the /usr/local namespace by default).

Afterwards enter the following command to build and install dmenu
(if necessary as root):

    make clean install


Running dmenu
-------------
dmenu reads a list of items from stdin, shows them as a menu and prints the
chosen item to stdout:

    ls | dmenu -i -l 10 -p "open:"

With -A it lists the installed desktop applications instead and launches the
one you choose. See the man page (man dmenu) for the full details.


Options
-------
    dmenu [-AbCifLPv] [-l lines] [-p prompt] [-fn font] [-m monitor]
          [-x xoffset] [-y yoffset] [-z width] [-bw borderwidth]
          [-bc color] [-lc color] [-op opacity] [-ip padding]
          [-nb color] [-nf color] [-sb color] [-sf color]
          [-ob color] [-of color] [-w windowid]

Behaviour:

  -A              List the desktop applications from $XDG_DATA_HOME/applications
                  and $XDG_DATA_DIRS/*/applications instead of reading stdin.
                  Return launches the selected application and exits;
                  Shift-Return prints its command line instead. Exec= is
                  split into arguments and run directly, without a shell.
                  Terminal apps are run through $TERMINAL (default xterm).
  -C              Close dmenu, like Escape, when a mouse button is clicked
                  outside of it (dmenu grabs the pointer while open).
  -f              Grab the keyboard before reading stdin if stdin is not a
                  tty. Faster, but locks up X until stdin reaches EOF.
  -i              Match menu items case insensitively.
  -v              Print version information and exit.
  -w windowid     Embed dmenu into the window with the given id.

Layout:

  -b              Show dmenu at the bottom of the screen.
  -l lines        List items vertically, with the given number of lines.
  -L              Draw a thin line between the items of a vertical list.
  -ip padding     Add this many pixels to the height of every item of a
                  vertical list (half above, half below the text).
  -m monitor      Show dmenu on this monitor (numbering starts at 0).
  -P              Show dmenu on the monitor under the mouse pointer instead of
                  the focused window's monitor. Ignored if -m is given.
  -x xoffset      Distance in pixels from the left edge of the monitor.
  -y yoffset      Distance in pixels from the top edge of the monitor (from the
                  bottom edge with -b), e.g. to place dmenu next to a bar.
  -z width        Width in pixels instead of the full monitor width.
  -bw borderwidth Draw a border of this width in pixels (included in -z).
  -op opacity     Window opacity in percent, 0 to 100 (default 100). Needs a
                  compositor that supports _NET_WM_WINDOW_OPACITY, such as
                  picom.

Text and colors (#RGB, #RRGGBB and X color names are accepted):

  -p prompt       Prompt shown to the left of the input field.
  -fn font        Font or font set to use.
  -nb color       Normal background.
  -nf color       Normal foreground.
  -sb color       Selected background.
  -sf color       Selected foreground.
  -ob color       Outline background (multiple selection).
  -of color       Outline foreground (multiple selection).
  -bc color       Border color (default: the selected background).
  -lc color       Color of the lines drawn by -L.
